    <div class="titleblock">
      <div><span class="k">Drawn by</span><span class="v">L. Veres</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Firm</span><span class="v">Veres Enterprises Inc.</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Sheet</span><span class="v">Home — 01</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Built for</span><span class="v">Prairie SMB contractors</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="problem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Where projects lose time</h2>
    <p class="lede">The schedule is only useful if it reflects what's actually happening on site. Most of the time, it doesn't — not because anyone is careless, but because updates travel slowly: a text here, a phone call there, a photo nobody logs anywhere central.</p>

    <div class="problem-grid">
      <div class="problem-panel">
        <span class="tag scheduled">As scheduled</span>
        <ul>
          <li>Foundation inspection <span class="d">Day 12</span></li>
          <li>Framing complete <span class="d">Day 28</span></li>
          <li>Rough-in handoff <span class="d">Day 34</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="problem-panel">
        <span class="tag built">As built</span>
        <ul>
          <li>Foundation inspection <span class="d">Day 14</span></li>
          <li>Framing complete <span class="d">Day 33</span></li>
          <li>Rough-in handoff <span class="d">— pending</span></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="problem-note">By the time someone manually reconciles the two, the gap has usually already cost something — a missed trade handoff, a client update that turns out to be wrong, or a dispute nobody saw coming.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="on-navy" id="product">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>How SaaFlow closes the gap</h2>
    <p class="lede">No new system for the field to learn. SaaFlow works with how crews and PMs already communicate, and does the reconciling for you.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <span class="num">01</span>
        <h3>Capture</h3>
        <p>Field updates come in as a text, a photo, or a quick voice note — whatever the crew is already using.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="num">02</span>
        <h3>Reconcile</h3>
        <p>SaaFlow matches those updates against the master schedule and flags drift automatically, before it compounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <span class="num">03</span>
        <h3>Inform</h3>
        <p>Every stakeholder — PM, GC, client — sees the same accurate picture, without a status-update meeting.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="positioning">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="position-grid">
      <div>
        <h2>Built for how Prairie contractors actually work</h2>
        <p class="lede">Most scheduling software is built for firms with a dedicated planning department and a six-figure software line item. Saskatchewan, Alberta, and Manitoba are full of small and mid-size GCs doing the real building without either — SaaFlow is priced and designed for them specifically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="compare">
        <div class="headrow">
          <div class="head">Enterprise tools</div>
          <div class="head" style="color:var(--teal-deep)">SaaFlow</div>
        </div>
        <div class="row"><div>Built for planning teams</div><div>Built for working PMs</div></div>
        <div class="row"><div>Long implementation</div><div>Runs on day one</div></div>
        <div class="row"><div>Enterprise pricing</div><div>Priced for SMB contractors</div></div>
        <div class="row"><div>New app for the field</div><div>Uses existing habits</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="on-navy" id="consulting">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="consult-grid">
      <div>
        <h2>Consulting, while we build</h2>
        <p class="lede">Alongside SaaFlow, Veres Enterprises offers hands-on project management consulting for small and mid-size construction firms — from someone who has run these projects, not just studied them.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:28px;">
          <a href="#contact" class="btn btn-primary">Talk about a project</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="service-list">
        <li>
          <span class="mark"></span>
          <div><h4>Schedule recovery & delay analysis</h4><p>Get a slipping project back to a schedule people can trust.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mark"></span>
          <div><h4>Project controls setup</h4><p>Practical reporting and tracking that a small team can actually keep up.</p></div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="mark"></span>
          <div><h4>PM process & reporting audits</h4><p>A clear-eyed look at where updates get lost and what to fix first.</p></div>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="about">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="about-inner">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="n">10+</span>
        <span class="l">Years managing residential & mixed-use construction</span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>Built by someone who's lived the gap</h2>
        <p>Veres Enterprises is led by Leslie, a construction project manager with over a decade of experience delivering residential and mixed-use developments, holding an MSc, MBA, and MPA.</p>
        <p>SaaFlow didn't start as a product idea — it started as a recurring frustration: watching accurate field information arrive too late to actually change an outcome. Everything here is built to fix that, first for the projects Leslie runs, then for every PM working the same gap.</p>
      </div>
